# #4102 — every row carries its owner. Rows with no owner cannot be written
# through the door at all (a role's token signs its own rows), so they were
# loaded straight from this file, around the API. Jeff, 2026-09-04: cards,
# practices and principles are Wren's.
# #4006 — the 14 XP principles, the second half of the loom's principle layer.
#
# The team already served the 14 permaculture principles it DESIGNS by. These
# are the 14 it BUILDS by (Beck, Extreme Programming Explained, 2nd ed., ch. 3),
# and they were a decision from 2026-08-05 that nobody carded, so nothing could
# cite them: /principles served 14, principleKind on zero, and Kade's Practice
# work (#3754) had no XP principle IRI to bind expresses→ to.
#
# Each carries the same two readings the PC set carries — techReading (how the
# principle shows up in this system's mechanisms) and jeffReading (how it shows
# up in how Jeff actually works). Written from this team's own receipts rather
# than paraphrased from the book, because a principle nobody can point at in
# our practice is decoration.

@prefix chorus: <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

chorus:xp-humanity a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Humanity" ;
    rdfs:comment "Software is written by people. To be effective, a process must acknowledge human needs — safety, accomplishment, belonging, growth, intimacy — and the tension between what a person needs and what a team needs." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Roles are not interchangeable executors. Andon state, the attention contract, and the 60-second pair heartbeat exist because a stalled role is a person waiting, not a queue entry." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff names tone as product. When tone drops, his cognitive budget goes to stabilising instead of creating — a humanity cost measured in the work that did not get made." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-economics a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Economics" ;
    rdfs:comment "Someone has to pay. Software development that does not serve economic needs dies. Time value of money and the option value of systems are the two economic drivers that matter most." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Opportunity cost is a first-class card axis. Cost logging at breakpoints, the cost dashboard, WSJF smallest-first when priority ties." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff cost-killed hosted CI on 2026-04-29 rather than carry a lane nobody read. The decision was economic, stated as such, and recorded." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-mutual-benefit a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Mutual Benefit" ;
    rdfs:comment "Every activity should benefit all concerned. Solutions that benefit me now at your expense, or me now at my own expense later, are the most destructive form of waste." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "A brief written for a peer is also the record for the future reader. Tests that describe Jeff's experience serve the author, the reviewer, and the person debugging in six weeks." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff refuses fixes that buy today at tomorrow's expense — the 'cards are not fixes' correction is exactly this principle." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-self-similarity a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Self-Similarity" ;
    rdfs:comment "Try copying the structure of one solution into a new context, even at a different scale. The details will be wrong; the shape is often right." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "The same shape recurs: value stream → domain → service → product. The werk pipeline mirrors the card lifecycle mirrors the session lifecycle." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff sees pattern before detail — HBDI-D. Self-similarity is how he reads a new problem: what does this rhyme with?" ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-improvement a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Improvement" ;
    rdfs:comment "There is no perfect process. Excellence in software development comes through a process of improvement, not through perfection at the outset. Make it work, make it right, make it fast — in that order." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Ship small, then ratchet. The lint ratchet, the coverage floor, the zero-red nightly bar are improvement encoded as a mechanism rather than as an intention." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "PDCA is Jeff's native loop: ideate, do, assess, adjust. He is comfortable acting on incomplete information because the assess step is real." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-diversity a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Diversity" ;
    rdfs:comment "Teams need to bring together a variety of skills, attitudes and perspectives to see problems and pitfalls, to think of multiple ways to solve problems, and to implement the solutions." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Three roles with different lenses on the same card. Peer review that asks for corrections, not agreement. The judge-panel shape in review work." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff asks all three roles and composes the answers rather than directing one — he is buying perspective diversity on purpose." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-reflection a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Reflection" ;
    rdfs:comment "Good teams do not just do their work, they think about how they are working and why they are working. They analyse why they succeeded or failed, and hide neither mistakes nor problems." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Retros, the introspection step at session close, the spine as an unrotated memory layer. Defects named in commit messages rather than quietly patched." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "'The process is the product.' Jeff treats how we build as first-class output, not overhead." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-flow a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Flow" ;
    rdfs:comment "Deliver a steady flow of valuable software by engaging in all the activities of development simultaneously. Flow suggests deploying smaller increments of value ever more frequently." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "One card at a time to WIP, land when green, no batching. The werk pipeline is a flow instrument: commit → test → deploy → demo → land, every card, same path." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff's two-touch coordination target per card is a flow constraint — every extra touch is flow interrupted." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-opportunity a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Opportunity" ;
    rdfs:comment "Learn to see problems as opportunities for change. Problems are not just occasions to survive; they are chances to transform the team's practice." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "A defect surfaces a missing gate. A flaky test surfaces an unstated tolerance. The card is not the fix — the mechanism the defect revealed is." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff turns friction into structure: the attention contract, the bouncer, the negative-proof rule all began as a bad afternoon." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-redundancy a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Redundancy" ;
    rdfs:comment "Critical, difficult problems should be solved several different ways. Then if one solution fails, the others prevent disaster." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Nudges persist AND deliver (DEC-107). Pre-commit hooks, role gates, and the merge gate answer different questions about the same change." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff locked the two-path nudge as a decision after it was reopened four times — redundancy chosen deliberately, not by accident." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-failure a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Failure" ;
    rdfs:comment "When you do not know what to do, risking failure can be the shortest, surest road to success. Failure is not waste if it imparts knowledge." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Spikes are carded as spikes. A negative proof is a deliberate failure, run on purpose, to prove a check can go red." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff tags work as spike / discovery / commitment so a failed spike reads as learning rather than as a miss." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-quality a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Quality" ;
    rdfs:comment "Quality is not a purchase decision — you do not get to choose between quality and speed. Projects do not go faster by accepting lower quality, and they do not go slower by demanding higher quality." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Done means no new defects. TDD before code. The zero-red nightly bar with no categories and no victory laps." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff: 'just about done having cards finish with defects.' He has never once traded quality for a date." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-baby-steps a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Baby Steps" ;
    rdfs:comment "What is the least you could do that is recognisably in the right direction? Big changes made all at once are dangerous; small steps let you back out of a mistake cheaply." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "Ship small. Smallest card first at equal priority. A werk lands one card, not a batch." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff moves fast and course-corrects in motion — small steps are what make correcting cheap enough to do continuously." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

chorus:xp-accepted-responsibility a chorus:Principle ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    rdfs:label "Accepted Responsibility" ;
    rdfs:comment "Responsibility cannot be assigned; it can only be accepted. If someone tries to give you responsibility, only you can decide whether you are responsible or not." ;
    dcterms:source "Beck, K. with Andres, C. Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change, 2nd ed. (Addison-Wesley, 2004), ch. 3." ;
    chorus:techReading "A role owns its domain and its ops failures. 'Own ops failures — start fixing immediately' is this principle as a standing rule." ;
    chorus:jeffReading "Jeff assigns lanes but never assigns caring. A role that treats its own red as someone else's problem has declined the responsibility, not lacked it." ;
    chorus:principleKind "xp" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    dcterms:modified "2026-08-25T13:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime .
